# Counter-flow heat exchanger — two channels running 0.4 mm apart, opposed.
#
# This example exists to show a design whose checks you ACCEPT rather than fix.
# Two independent networks and a 0.4 mm wall between them are both flagged, and
# both are the point of the device: heat has to cross that wall, and the two
# streams must never meet. OpenChip never blocks an export — it tells you what
# it sees and leaves the call to you.
#
# Expected report: 8 cautions (7 × channels-too-close, 1 × disconnected-network).

chip "Counter-flow exchanger"
printer standard-4k
size 40 x 60
base 4
wall 2
edge-spacing 3
split two-piece at 2
channel 0.8 x 0.5 rect

# Hot side runs left to right, at 60 °C.
port hotIn  at 6,30  luer-slip inlet water 10 at 60C
port hotOut at 34,30 luer-slip outlet
path hotIn -> (14,30) -> (26,30) -> hotOut

# Cold side runs right to left, 1.2 mm away — 0.4 mm of wall between the two
# 0.8 mm channels. Thin on purpose: wall thickness is the whole resistance.
port coldIn  at 34,31.2 luer-slip inlet glycerol 10 at 20C
port coldOut at 6,31.2  luer-slip outlet
path coldIn -> (26,31.2) -> (14,31.2) -> coldOut
